var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"design/#Design","page":"Design","text":"This page is the design of TableOps.jl: what it does, how each verb behaves and why. It is the reference for contributors. Where a rule rests on published work, the References section lists the source; rules with no such support are marked (own choice).","title":"Design"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Goals","page":"Design","text":"TableOps brings tidyverse-style data manipulation to Julia: easy to read, fast, and correct. Six principles decide every design question, in this order when they conflict:\n\nScientifically, statistically and methodologically correct. No result is silently wrong. Missing values are never dropped or invented without the user asking.\nA good user experience. One clear name per operation, safe defaults, errors that say what went wrong and how to fix it.\nFast. Quick to load, quick on the first call, quick on large tables.\nLight. Pure Julia; Tables.jl is the only dependency.\nCompatible. Any Tables.jl table goes in; every result is a Tables.jl table, so CSV.jl, Arrow.jl, DataFrames.jl and the rest work with it.\nJulia 1.10 and later.\n\nThe names follow the tidyverse and SQL. The implementation is original: no code is read from, translated from or modelled on another package.","title":"Goals"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#A-first-example","page":"Design","text":"using TableOps\n\ndt = (store = [\"A\", \"A\", \"B\"], date = [1, 2, 1], revenue = [10.0, 20.0, 5.0])\n\n# Macro form: bare column names.\n@select(dt, store, date, revenue) |> @group_by(store) |>\n    @summarize(store_revenue = sum(revenue))\n\n# Function form: the same result.\nselect(dt, :store, :date, :revenue) |> group_by(:store) |>\n    summarize(store_revenue = :revenue => sum)","title":"A first example"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Scope","page":"Design","text":"Version 0.1 contains everything below.\n\nArea Functions\nTable Table, as_table, glimpse, nrow, ncol, names, copy, printing\nColumns select, rename, relocate, mutate, pull\nColumn pickers any name predicate (startswith(\"x\"), endswith, contains with a string or a Regex, a function), everything(), of_type(T), except(cols...)\nRows where, arrange, desc, distinct, slice, slice_head, slice_tail, slice_min, slice_max, slice_sample\nGroups GroupedTable, group_by, ungroup, summarize (alias summarise), tally, across\nHelpers n_distinct, lag, lead, row_number, min_rank, dense_rank, percent_rank, cume_dist, ntile, between, case_when, each (and n() inside macros)\nWindows rolling_sum, rolling_mean, rolling_min, rolling_max, rolling_var, rolling_std, rolling\nJoins inner_join, left_join, right_join, full_join, semi_join, anti_join, cross_join\nCombine bind_rows, bind_cols, union, intersect, setdiff\nReshape pivot_longer, pivot_wider, separate, unite\nMissing values drop_missing, replace_missing, fill_missing, complete\nNesting nest, unnest\nPanels PanelTable, panel_id, panel_time, nseries, series_ids, has_gaps, fill_gaps\nMacros one per verb: @select, @where, @mutate, @summarize, …\nExtension AbstractSelector, resolve, window_function, grouped_reduce\n\nOut of scope: reading and writing files (CSV.jl, Arrow.jl and others already produce Tables.jl tables), plotting, and SQL strings.\n\nJulia already has ifelse, coalesce, cumsum, accumulate, skipmissing, startswith, endswith and contains; TableOps uses them and does not add its own versions.\n\nThe extension functions are exported because Julia 1.10 has no public keyword.","title":"Scope"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Statistics","page":"Design","text":"mean, median, var, std and quantile live in the Statistics standard library. TableOps does not depend on it: a package extension ([weakdeps] Statistics) adds the single-pass grouped_reduce methods for mean, var and std when the user loads Statistics. Without it, any function still works through the generic per-group path.","title":"Statistics"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#SQL-to-TableOps","page":"Design","text":"SQL TableOps\nSELECT a, b select\nWHERE where\nGROUP BY group_by\nSUM(), AVG(), COUNT(*) summarize, tally\nHAVING where after summarize\nORDER BY arrange\nSELECT DISTINCT distinct\nLIMIT slice_head\nINNER/LEFT/RIGHT/FULL JOIN inner_join, left_join, right_join, full_join\nCROSS JOIN cross_join\nWHERE x IN (SELECT …) semi_join\nWHERE x NOT IN (SELECT …) anti_join\nUNION ALL / UNION bind_rows / union\nINTERSECT / EXCEPT intersect / setdiff\nCASE WHEN case_when\nCOALESCE coalesce.\nwindow LAG, LEAD, ROW_NUMBER, RANK lag, lead, row_number, min_rank","title":"SQL to TableOps"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Core-types","page":"Design","text":"","title":"Core types"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Table","page":"Design","text":"An immutable table that stores its columns without type parameters:\n\nnames::Vector{Symbol}\ncolumns::Vector{AbstractVector}\na Dict{Symbol,Int} from name to position\n\nAll columns have the same length and all names are unique; the constructor checks both. The storage is deliberately untyped so that a verb that changes the set of columns does not trigger new compilation (see Performance).\n\nTable(x) accepts any Tables.jl table (column or row access) and keeps the source's column vectors as they are, without copying.\nTable(; a = [1, 2], b = [\"x\", \"y\"]) builds a table from keyword columns.\nt.revenue returns a column, whatever the column is called (internal code uses getfield, so a column named names or columns is fine). names(t) returns a Vector{Symbol} (own choice, as Tables.jl); nrow(t) and ncol(t) give the size.\ncopy(t) copies every column, so changing a column of the copy leaves t unchanged. The copy is shallow: mutable values inside cells (a vector in a cell, say) are shared.\nTable implements the Tables.jl column interface (Tables.istable, Tables.columnaccess, Tables.columns, Tables.getcolumn, Tables.columnnames, Tables.schema).\nPrinting shows the size, each column's name and element type, and the first and last rows, cut to the display size. glimpse(t) shows one line per column.\n\nColumns a verb creates are plain Vectors with a specific element type, never Vector{Any}; a column that holds missing is Vector{Union{Missing,T}}.","title":"Table"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#GroupedTable","page":"Design","text":"A Table (or a PanelTable) plus its group columns and a group index computed once by group_by:\n\nthe group number of every row,\nthe rows ordered by group,\nthe first row of each group in that order,\nthe number of groups.\n\nsummarize, mutate, where, the slice_* verbs and the window helpers reuse this index; none recomputes it. ungroup returns the parent table.","title":"GroupedTable"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#PanelTable","page":"Design","text":"A table of several time series: a Table plus its id columns (zero or more; zero means a single series) and one time column. See Panels.","title":"PanelTable"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#The-three-forms-of-every-verb","page":"Design","text":"Table first: select(dt, :store, :date). dt may be any Tables.jl table; anything other than a TableOps type is wrapped with Table(dt).\nPipe: select(:store, :date) returns a function of a table, so dt |> select(:store) |> group_by(:store) works.\nMacro: @select(dt, store, date) or dt |> @select(store, date).\n\nThe table-first and pipe forms are told apart by dispatch on the first argument: a TableOps type, or any x with Tables.istable(x), is the table. A join or bind_rows with a single table argument is the pipe form: x |> left_join(y; by = :id).\n\nException: the set operations union, intersect and setdiff extend Julia's own functions for TableOps tables and have no pipe form, because union(x) already has a meaning in Julia.\n\nThe macros only rewrite their arguments into the function form; they add no behaviour of their own.","title":"The three forms of every verb"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Column-specifications-in-the-function-form","page":"Design","text":"A function always receives whole columns:\n\nSpec Meaning\n:revenue the column\n:revenue => sum sum(revenue)\n[:revenue, :cost] => (r, c) -> r .- c a function of several columns\nname = spec the result is called name (in mutate, summarize)\nname = :other a copy of column other under a new name\nname = vector a new column, if its length is the row count (mutate)\nname = value any other value is a constant, repeated for every row (mutate)\nname = t -> … a function of the (group's) sub-table\n\neach(f) applies f element by element, for short elementwise specs: where(t, :revenue => each(>(100))), mutate(t, r = :revenue => each(round)) (own choice).\n\nColumn names may be Symbols or Strings in pickers and on the left of a =>. On the right of name =, only a Symbol names a column: a String there is a constant (mutate(t, region = \"EU\")). A vector whose length is neither 1 nor the row count is an error.","title":"Column specifications in the function form"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Column-pickers","page":"Design","text":"Wherever columns are chosen (select, across, relocate, pivot_longer, drop_missing, …):\n\na name or a vector of names;\na predicate on the column name, such as Julia's startswith(\"rev\"), endswith(\"_x\"), contains(\"sales\"), contains(r\"^q[1-4]$\"), or any function String -> Bool;\neverything(); of_type(T) (columns whose element type, ignoring missing, is a subtype of T); except(cols...) (all columns but these).\n\nselect keeps the order in which columns are picked; a column picked twice appears once, at its first position. select(t, new = :old) renames while selecting.","title":"Column pickers"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Signatures","page":"Design","text":"The table-first form of each verb; the pipe form drops t.\n\nselect(t, cols...; new = :old, …)        rename(t; new = :old, …)\nrelocate(t, cols...; before = nothing, after = nothing)   # default: to the front\npull(t, col)                             mutate(t, specs...; name = spec, …)\nwhere(t, specs...)                       # several conditions are combined with AND\narrange(t, keys...; by_group = false)    desc(col)\ndistinct(t, cols...; keep_all = false)\nslice(t, rows)                           slice_head(t; n)    slice_tail(t; n)\nslice_min(t, col; n = 1, with_ties = true)   slice_max(t, col; n = 1, with_ties = true)\nslice_sample(t; n, rng, replace = false)\ngroup_by(t, cols...; sort = true)        ungroup(gt)\nsummarize(t, specs...; name = spec, …)   tally(t, cols...; name = :n)\nacross(cols, fns; names = \"{col}_{fn}\")  # fns: a function, a vector or a NamedTuple\nlag(x, k = 1; default = missing)         lead(x, k = 1; default = missing)\nrow_number(x)   min_rank(x)   dense_rank(x)   percent_rank(x)   cume_dist(x)\nntile(x, k)     between(x, lo, hi)       n_distinct(xs...)\ncase_when(cond => value, …; default = missing)   each(f)\nrolling_mean(x, k; partial = false)      rolling(f, x, k; partial = false)\ninner_join(x, y; by, relationship = nothing, match_missing = false, suffix = (\"_x\", \"_y\"))\ncross_join(x, y; suffix = (\"_x\", \"_y\"))\nbind_rows(ts...; id = nothing)           bind_cols(ts...)\n\nslice_min / slice_max with with_ties = true keep every row tied with the n-th value. bind_rows(…; id = :source) adds a column naming the source table (its position, or the keyword name when tables are given as name = t).","title":"Signatures"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Macros","page":"Design","text":"You write It becomes\nrevenue the column revenue if the table has one, otherwise the variable revenue\n$threshold always the variable threshold\n:revenue always the column revenue\narithmetic and comparison operators: + - * / ^ % ÷ == != ≠ < <= ≤ > >= ≥, !, unary - elementwise: .+, .==, .<, .!, …\na && b, a || b a .& b, a .| b (three-valued with missing)\na < b < c (a .< b) .& (b .< c)\nc ? a : b ifelse.(c, a, b)\nx in s, x ∈ s in.(x, Ref(s)): membership of each element in s\n===, !== unchanged (identity, not elementwise)\nsum(revenue), myfun(x) unchanged: the function gets whole columns\nlog.(revenue) unchanged: elementwise because the user wrote the dot\nn() the number of rows in the current group (macros only; n is not exported, so users keep n as a variable name; the function form writes t -> nrow(t))\nvar\"unit price\" the column named unit price\n-store (in a picker) except(:store)\ndesc(revenue) desc(:revenue)\n\nOperators are elementwise; functions are not. A function that is not elementwise by construction gets the dot from the user: log.(revenue). This is Julia's own rule, so a mistake is an error, never a silently wrong result.\nWhether a bare name is a column or a variable is decided when the code runs: the macro cannot know the table's columns when the code is written. A column wins over a variable of the same name; $x forces the variable.\nNames bound inside the expression are not rewritten: lambda arguments (x -> x + 1), comprehension and generator variables, let bindings, and keyword names in calls (round.(x; digits = 2)).\nIn @select(dt, store) the first argument is the table if it is a bare name of a defined variable (@isdefined) that holds a table when the code runs; otherwise every argument is a column and the macro returns the pipe form. Corner case: if a variable holding a table has the same name as a column the user means, writing :name forces the column.\nwhere (x) with a space before the parenthesis is a Julia parse error; the docs always write where(x).","title":"Macros"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Behaviour","page":"Design","text":"","title":"Behaviour"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Missing-values","page":"Design","text":"Calculations propagate missing. sum([1, missing]) is missing. To skip missing values the user says so: sum(skipmissing(revenue)). Dropping them silently would be a hidden assumption about why they are missing.\nwhere keeps only rows where the condition is true. false and missing rows are dropped, as SQL WHERE does. A condition that is not Bool or missing is an error. Note for the docs: !(x == 1) also drops rows where x is missing.\nGrouping: all missing keys form one group, listed last. distinct and tally treat missing the same way, so the verbs agree (own choice).\nJoins: missing keys never match (as SQL NULL); match_missing = true makes them match. NaN keys are ordinary values and match each other.\nKey equality (grouping, distinct, joins, set operations) is Julia's isequal, except that -0.0 and 0.0 are the same key, as they are the same number (own choice).\nPivots: pivot_wider writes missing for absent combinations (values_fill = sets another value); pivot_longer keeps missing values (drop_missing = true removes them). Implicit missing values become explicit, never silently lost.","title":"Missing values"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Order","page":"Design","text":"Every verb has a documented, deterministic output order.\n\narrange is a stable sort. missing sorts last, also with desc (own choice). NaN follows Julia's isless (after +Inf).\narrange on a grouped table sorts the whole table and ignores the groups unless by_group = true (own choice).\nGroups are sorted by key; group_by(…; sort = false) keeps the order of first appearance.\ndistinct keeps the first occurrence of each row, in the original order. distinct(t, :a, :b) returns those columns; keep_all = true keeps the rest.\nJoins keep the left table's row order; for each left row, matches come in the right table's order. Unmatched right rows (right_join, full_join) follow, in the right table's order.","title":"Order"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Lengths-and-types","page":"Design","text":"In mutate, a result of length 1 is repeated for every row; any other length that is not the table's (or group's) row count is an error showing both lengths (own choice).\nIn summarize, each result must be a single value per group; otherwise the error suggests mutate. summarize returns an ungrouped Table (a PanelTable for panels, see below), with the group columns first (own choice).\nsummarize on a table with no rows and no groups calls each function on the empty columns and returns one row; with groups it returns no rows. If a function fails on empty input (such as maximum), the error names the column and the group and suggests init =.\nNo silent type conversions. Combining columns (bind_rows, pivot_longer, joins, case_when, replace_missing) follows one rule (own choice):\nintegers combine to the wider integer type;\nintegers and floats combine to a float of at least Float64 (Int64 with Float32 gives Float64, not Float32);\nBool does not combine with numbers;\nany other mix (a String with an Int, say) is an error naming the column and both types.","title":"Lengths and types"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Names","page":"Design","text":"Duplicate column names are an error.\nIn joins, non-key columns that exist on both sides get the suffixes suffix = (\"_x\", \"_y\") (own choice).\nbind_cols with a duplicate name is an error.\nrename(t, new = :old); var\"new name\" = :old for names with spaces.","title":"Names"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Joins","page":"Design","text":"by is required: by = :id, by = [:a, :b], or by = [:a => :b] when the key has a different name on the right. Without it the error lists the columns the two tables share (own choice: no silent natural join).\nMany-to-many joins must be declared. By default a join where a key value is duplicated on both sides is an error showing one such key, because it multiplies rows, a common source of silently wrong results. Declare relationship = :many_to_many to allow it. :one_to_one, :one_to_many and :many_to_one are checked against the data when given; the first word is the left table (:one_to_many: left keys unique, right keys may repeat).\nOutput columns: the left table's columns, then the right table's non-key columns. Key columns appear once, under the left names. semi_join and anti_join return the left columns only.","title":"Joins"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Memory","page":"Design","text":"Verbs never modify their input. Unchanged columns are shared with the input rather than copied (select copies nothing; mutate creates only the new columns). Consequence, documented: changing a source vector in place afterwards also changes the table; copy(t) gives a table whose columns are independent of the source (a shallow copy; see Core types).","title":"Memory"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Randomness","page":"Design","text":"slice_sample(t; n, rng) requires rng, so every sample can be reproduced (own choice; also avoids a dependency on Random).","title":"Randomness"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Panels","page":"Design","text":"p = PanelTable(dt; id = :store, time = :date)     # id may be [:store, :region]\np |> @mutate(prev = lag(sales), avg7 = rolling_mean(sales, 7))\np |> group_by(:region) |> summarize(sales = sum(sales))   # new panel, id = :region\n\nConstruction checks that each (id, time) pair is unique (an error shows the first duplicate), that time has no missing, and sorts rows by id, then time. Gaps are allowed; they are never filled automatically.\nGaps are explicit: has_gaps(p; step) and fill_gaps(p; step, fill). step is given by the user (Day(1), Month(1), 1). The regular grid of a series is t0 + k * step for k = 0, 1, … (not repeated +, which would drift on month ends); only +, * with an integer and isless are used, so Dates is not a dependency.\nAn ungrouped panel behaves as grouped by its id. Every mutate and where spec runs per series, in time order: lag, cumsum, rolling windows and any function a user writes see one series at a time and never cross series. To compute across series, the user groups explicitly or calls as_table first.\ngroup_by on a panel is always allowed and keeps the panel. Specs then run per group. When the groups mix several series (for example grouping by :region when the id is :store), a window helper (window_function(f) is true) is an error: lags must not cross series, so the message suggests grouping by the id or summarizing to the new level first. A user function that is not marked as a window function cannot be detected; the docs say so and recommend the window helpers.\nsummarize on a panel summarizes per group and per time point. The result is a new PanelTable whose id is the group columns (no groups: a single series). For one row per series over the whole period, the user writes as_table(p) |> group_by(:store) |> summarize(…).\nThe general rule: a verb returns a PanelTable when its result still has the id and time columns unchanged and every (id, time) pair is still unique. Verbs that can duplicate rows (slice_sample(…; replace = true), joins, bind_rows) check uniqueness again and raise an error naming the duplicate and the fix.\nErrors at the verb that would break the panel, naming the fix (as_table(p) first): select or distinct without the id or time columns, a mutate, separate or unite that overwrites or removes them, and arrange. rename (also select(new = :id)) of an id or time column updates the panel.\nVerbs that keep the panel: where, mutate, relocate, slice_*, distinct, select, bind_cols, separate, unite, drop_missing, replace_missing, fill_missing, inner_join, left_join, semi_join, anti_join, bind_rows of panels with the same id and time, and fill_gaps.\nVerbs that return a plain Table: right_join, full_join, cross_join, the set operations, pivot_longer, pivot_wider, complete (use fill_gaps for time), tally, nest, unnest, as_table.\nNo warnings. Every problem is an error at the point where it happens; correct code runs without noise.\n\npanel_id(p) and panel_time(p) are the public accessors other packages (such as MachineLearningForecast.jl or Durbyn.jl, through a package extension) can use later.","title":"Panels"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Reshaping,-missing-values,-nesting","page":"Design","text":"pivot_longer(t, cols; names_to = :name, values_to = :value, drop_missing = false).\npivot_wider(t; names_from, values_from, id_cols = everything else, values_fill = missing, values_fn = nothing). A duplicated (id, name) pair is an error showing it, unless values_fn (for example sum) says how to combine.\nseparate(t, col; into, delim, remove = true): a value with the wrong number of pieces is an error showing the row, unless too_few = :missing or too_many = :merge / :drop is given.\nunite(t, new, cols...; sep = \"_\", remove = true, skip_missing = false): with a missing piece the result is missing unless skip_missing = true.\ndrop_missing(t, cols...) (all columns if none given), replace_missing(t; col = value, …), fill_missing(t, cols...; direction = :down) (:up, :downup, :updown), per group or per series.\ncomplete(t, cols...; fill = (;)) adds every combination of the distinct values of cols that is absent, with missing (or fill) elsewhere.\nnest(gt; name = :data) turns each group into one row with a Table column; unnest(t, :data) reverses it.","title":"Reshaping, missing values, nesting"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Windows","page":"Design","text":"rolling_mean(x, k) and the others are trailing windows: the value at row i uses rows i-k+1 to i. The first k-1 values are missing unless partial = true. rolling(f, x, k) applies any function to each window. Sums, means, minima and maxima run in O(n) (running sums, a monotonic queue); variance and standard deviation use a numerically stable update and are tested against direct computation. For forecasting features, combine with lag so that the window ends before the current row.","title":"Windows"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Errors","page":"Design","text":"Every check throws an ArgumentError that states:\n\nwhat was wrong, in which verb,\nthe offending value,\nthe valid options or the fix, kept separate from the explanation.\n\n\"Did you mean revenue?\" is added only when exactly one column name is a close match (small edit distance); a guessed fix that is wrong misleads more than no fix. TableOps never prints warnings.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Performance","page":"Design","text":"","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Targets","page":"Design","text":"Measured on a 2-core machine, for the common verbs; a release must meet them.\n\nWhat Target\nusing TableOps under 0.5 s\nfirst call of a typical chain under 1 s\none verb on up to 1 million rows, compiled under 100 ms\nagainst DataFrames.jl on the same task not slower\n\nDelays above about 100 ms are noticeable, and an added 500 ms per operation measurably reduces how much people explore their data.","title":"Targets"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#How","page":"Design","text":"Function barriers. The table is untyped; every inner loop is a separate function that receives concrete column types and is type-stable (checked with @inferred in the tests).\nKey encoding. Each key column is encoded once into integer codes (a hash map in general, a direct lookup for small integer ranges). Several keys combine into one integer when the product of their cardinalities fits, otherwise into a hashed tuple of codes. A counting pass then orders the rows by group in O(n); only the distinct keys are sorted.\nSingle-pass reductions. sum, length (and n()), minimum, maximum, first, last and n_distinct run in one pass over the rows into per-group results; with Statistics loaded, so do mean, var and std (Welford's method, through the extension). Any other function runs on a view of each group. A new fast path is one method of grouped_reduce.\nGather once. where, slice_* and joins build a row index first, then copy each column once; no verb iterates rows as NamedTuples.\nJoins are hash joins on the encoded keys, built on the smaller table.\nSorting uses Julia's stable sortperm; several keys are encoded first.\nThreads are used only when Julia has more than one and the input is large. Results are identical for every thread count: chunk sizes are fixed and partial results combine in a fixed order.\nLoad time. Tables.jl only; plain precompile statements for the common column types (Int, Float64, String, Bool and their missing unions); no method definitions that invalidate other code.","title":"How"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Benchmarks","page":"Design","text":"bench/ has its own environment (BenchmarkTools, DataFrames, CSV, Arrow), so none of these become package dependencies. Synthetic tables from 10³ to 10⁷ rows; load time, first-call time, run time and allocations; results in bench/results.md, rerun before each release and after every change aimed at speed.","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Extending-TableOps","page":"Design","text":"Extension is by adding methods, never by branching on a mode string; keyword options only choose among the documented behaviours of a verb.\n\nNew column picker: a subtype of AbstractSelector with a resolve(selector, t) method that returns the picked names; it may look at the names and the column types of the table t.\nNew window helper: a function of a whole column, registered the way lag is so that it runs per group and per series.\nNew fast reduction: a grouped_reduce(::typeof(f), column, groups) method; summarize(:x => f) then uses it.\nNew table types from other packages: methods for Table, GroupedTable or PanelTable, or any Tables.jl table, which already works as input.","title":"Extending TableOps"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Testing","page":"Design","text":"Test.jl, one test_<topic>.jl per source file (plus test_compat.jl and the shared checks.jl), all included from test/runtests.jl inside one @testset, then Aqua.jl. Random inputs use StableRNGs.\nExpected outputs are small tables written by hand in the tests. No other software is run to produce them.\nEvery verb: the normal case; edge cases (no rows, no columns, one row, missing, NaN, a column that is all missing, Unicode names and names with spaces, wrong input types); every ArgumentError with @test_throws and its message.\nShared checks for every verb: function, pipe and macro forms agree; ungrouped, grouped and panel behaviour as specified; the input is unchanged; result columns have concrete element types; inner functions pass @inferred.\nFast paths are checked on random tables against a deliberately simple reference written in the test (a loop and a Dict); the same comparison checks that one and several threads give identical results.\nCompatibility: input from and output to NamedTuple tables, row tables, CSV, Arrow and DataFrames (test-only dependencies).\nThe full suite on Julia 1.10 and the latest release, and the docs build (doctests and checkdocs = :exports), before every commit.","title":"Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#References","page":"Design","text":"Used to verify the decisions above.\n\nWickham, H. (2014). Tidy data. Journal of Statistical Software 59(10). doi:10.18637/jss.v059.i10\nWickham, H. (2011). The split-apply-combine strategy for data analysis. Journal of Statistical Software 40(1). doi:10.18637/jss.v040.i01\nPetersohn, D. et al. (2020). Towards scalable dataframe systems. PVLDB 13(12). doi:10.14778/3407790.3407807\nWang, E., Cook, D. & Hyndman, R. J. (2020). A new tidy data structure to support exploration and modeling of temporal data. JCGS 29(3). doi:10.1080/10618600.2019.1695624\nRubin, D. B. (1976). Inference and missing data. Biometrika 63(3). doi:10.1093/biomet/63.3.581\nLibkin, L. (2016). SQL's three-valued logic and certain answers. ACM TODS 41(1). doi:10.1145/2877206\nLibkin, L. & Peterfreund, L. (2023). SQL nulls and two-valued logic. PODS. doi:10.1145/3584372.3588661\nToussaint, E., Guagliardo, P., Libkin, L. & Sequeda, J. (2022). Troubles with nulls, views from the users. PVLDB 15(11). doi:10.14778/3551793.3551818\nBrass, S. & Goldberg, C. (2006). Semantic errors in SQL queries: a quite complete list. J. Systems and Software 79(5). doi:10.1016/j.jss.2005.06.028\nIEEE Std 754-2019, IEEE Standard for Floating-Point Arithmetic. doi:10.1109/IEEESTD.2019.8766229\nGalappaththi, A., Nadi, S. & Treude, C. (2024). An empirical study of API misuses of data-centric libraries. ESEM. doi:10.1145/3674805.3686685\nPiccioni, M., Furia, C. A. & Meyer, B. (2013). An empirical study of API usability. ESEM. doi:10.1109/ESEM.2013.14\nBarik, T., Ford, D., Murphy-Hill, E. & Parnin, C. (2018). How should compilers explain problems to developers? ESEC/FSE. doi:10.1145/3236024.3236040\nMarceau, G., Fisler, K. & Krishnamurthi, S. (2011). Mind your language: on novices' interactions with error messages. Onward! doi:10.1145/2048237.2048241\nAnderson, B. B. et al. (2015). How polymorphic warnings reduce habituation in the brain. CHI. doi:10.1145/2702123.2702322\nJohnson, E. J. & Goldstein, D. (2003). Do defaults save lives? Science 302(5649). doi:10.1126/science.1091721\nCard, S. K., Robertson, G. G. & Mackinlay, J. D. (1991). The information visualizer, an information workspace. CHI. doi:10.1145/108844.108874\nLiu, Z. & Heer, J. (2014). The effects of interactive latency on exploratory visual analysis. IEEE TVCG 20(12). doi:10.1109/TVCG.2014.2346452","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#TableOps.jl","page":"Home","text":"Fast, tidyverse-style table verbs for any Tables.jl table, in pure Julia with Tables.jl as the only dependency.\n\nwarning: In development\nTableOps is being built; the planned API is in the Design page.","title":"TableOps.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Column-pickers","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Column pickers"},{"category":"module","location":"#TableOps","page":"Home","text":"TableOps\n\nFast, tidyverse-style table verbs for any Tables.jl table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps"},{"category":"type","location":"#TableOps.Table","page":"Home","text":"Table(x)\nTable(; name = column, ...)\n\nA table with named columns. Table(x) accepts any Tables.jl table, and Table(; a = [1, 2], b = [\"x\", \"y\"]) builds one from keyword columns, in keyword order.\n\nColumns are shared, not copied: Table(x) keeps the source's column vectors as they are, so changing a vector in place changes every table that holds it. A column that is not an AbstractVector is collected into one. A Table passed to Table is returned as is.\n\nt.revenue returns the stored column, whatever the column is called, and throws an ArgumentError with a suggestion for an unknown name. A Table cannot be changed in place: t.revenue = v throws. names(t) returns the column names as a Vector{Symbol} (a copy). copy(t) copies every column with copy, so the result is independent of t except for the elements themselves: a column of vectors shares its inner vectors. isequal compares names and columns with isequal; == follows Julia's three-valued rule and can return missing.\n\nEvery column must use 1-based indexing, all names must be unique, and all columns must have the same length; otherwise an ArgumentError says how to fix it.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> Table(; a = [1, 2], b = [\"x\", \"y\"])\n2×2 Table\n     a  b\n Int64  String\n ─────────────\n     1  x\n     2  y\n\nA Table prints its size, the column names and types and the first and last rows, fitted to the display; glimpse shows one line per column. In the REPL, rows and columns are fitted to the screen; written elsewhere, all rows and columns are shown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.Table"},{"category":"function","location":"#TableOps.as_table","page":"Home","text":"as_table(x)\n\nConvert x to a Table: a Table is returned as is, and any other Tables.jl table is wrapped as in Table(x).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> as_table((a = [1, 2],)) isa Table\ntrue\n\njulia> t = Table(; a = [1]); as_table(t) === t\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.as_table"},{"category":"function","location":"#TableOps.nrow","page":"Home","text":"nrow(t::Table)\n\nThe number of rows of t. A table with no columns has no rows.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> nrow(Table(; a = [1, 2, 3]))\n3\n\njulia> nrow(Table())\n0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.nrow"},{"category":"function","location":"#TableOps.ncol","page":"Home","text":"ncol(t::Table)\n\nThe number of columns of t.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> ncol(Table(; a = [1, 2, 3], b = [\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"]))\n2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.ncol"},{"category":"function","location":"#TableOps.glimpse","page":"Home","text":"glimpse(x)\nglimpse(io::IO, x)\n\nPrint a compact overview of the table x, any Tables.jl table: the size, then one line per column with its name, its element type and its first values, as many as fit in the width of io (the terminal for glimpse(x)). Returns nothing. Names, types and values wider than 20 columns are cut with …, and Union{Missing,T} is shown as T?.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> glimpse(Table(; a = [1, 2, 3], b = [\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"]))\n3×2 Table\n a  Int64   1, 2, 3\n b  String  x, y, z\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.glimpse"},{"category":"type","location":"#TableOps.AbstractSelector","page":"Home","text":"AbstractSelector\n\nThe supertype of column pickers such as everything(), of_type(T) and except(...). To add a picker, subtype it and add a method of the one public signature\n\nresolve(selector, t::Table)::Vector{Symbol}\n\nwhich returns the picked names, in order and without repeats. It may look at names(t) and at the element types of the columns of t.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> import TableOps: resolve\n\njulia> struct LastColumn <: AbstractSelector end\n\njulia> resolve(::LastColumn, t::Table) = [last(names(t))];\n\njulia> resolve(LastColumn(), Table(; a = [1], b = [2]))\n1-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :b\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.AbstractSelector"},{"category":"function","location":"#TableOps.resolve","page":"Home","text":"resolve(picker, t::Table)\n\nThe names of the columns of t that picker picks, as a Vector{Symbol}. A picker is a name (Symbol or String), a vector of names, a predicate on the column name as a String (startswith(\"re\"), endswith, contains, or any function that returns true or false), everything(), of_type(T), except(picks...) or a subtype of AbstractSelector. A name that is not a column is an error; a predicate that picks nothing gives an empty vector. A name in a vector appears once, at its first place.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> t = Table(; revenue = [1], region = [2], cost = [3]);\n\njulia> resolve(startswith(\"re\"), t)\n2-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :revenue\n :region\n\njulia> resolve(of_type(Int), t)\n3-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :revenue\n :region\n :cost\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.resolve"},{"category":"function","location":"#TableOps.everything","page":"Home","text":"everything()\n\nA column picker for every column of the table, in table order.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> resolve(everything(), Table(; a = [1], b = [2]))\n2-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :a\n :b\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.everything"},{"category":"function","location":"#TableOps.of_type","page":"Home","text":"of_type(T)\n\nA column picker for the columns whose element type is a subtype of T. missing is ignored: a Union{Missing,Float64} column is a Float64 column. A column that holds only missing has element type Missing and is picked by of_type(Missing) or of_type(Any) alone. Bool is an Integer, so of_type(Integer) picks Bool columns too; use of_type(Int) to avoid it.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> t = Table(; a = [1, 2], b = [1.5, missing], c = [\"x\", \"y\"]);\n\njulia> resolve(of_type(Real), t)\n2-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :a\n :b\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.of_type"},{"category":"function","location":"#TableOps.except","page":"Home","text":"except(picks...)\n\nA column picker for every column that none of picks picks. Each pick is any column picker; a name that is not a column is an error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using TableOps\n\njulia> resolve(except(:a), Table(; a = [1], b = [2], c = [3]))\n2-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :b\n :c\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TableOps.except"}]
}
